{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "mq4cs-gpt41-splade", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Certainly! The fingers are moved by the pull of muscles in the forearms through tendons that connect muscle to bone. These tendons run through a sheath that encases them, and this sheath has five thickened areas called annular pulleys (A1 to A5). The A2 and A4 pulleys are often associated with climbing injuries, such as climber's finger, which can cause painful swelling in the fingers, particularly the middle or ring finger.   There are no muscles in the fingers themselves; instead, the tendons there transfer force from the muscles in your forearms. Your symptoms might suggest a tendon-related issue, perhaps even related to the flexor tendon and its associated pulleys, given your climbing activities. It's important to rest and avoid any finger-intensive activity until you get a proper diagnosis. If pain persists, consider consulting a healthcare professional to ensure proper treatment and avoid further injury.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 3.8357599944879186, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 2.6131074231284264, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:0": 2.5776834593666305, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 2.3999229347645974, "clueweb22-en0012-45-12823:1": 2.2856573639818403, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:2": 2.2011499997699375, "clueweb22-en0034-76-16227:1": 2.050554004967586, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": 2.0229208286726506, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 1.9887721673589307, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 1.983422700305172}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 3.8357599944879186, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 2.6131074231284264, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:0": 2.5776834593666305, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 2.3999229347645974, "clueweb22-en0012-45-12823:1": 2.2856573