{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "mq4cs-gpt41-splade", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To tape effectively for a pulley injury like the one affecting your A2 pulley, you can use the H-taping method, which is particularly beneficial for supporting the tendons and preventing excessive finger flexion. Here\u2019s how you can do it:  1. **Cut the Tape**: Use rigid athletic tape, preferably around 1.5 cm wide. Cut a length about 10 cm long and split it in half lengthwise from both ends, leaving a 1 cm bridge in the middle.  2. **Position the Tape**: With your finger straight, place the bridge of the tape over the crease of the joint on the upper end of the injured pulley. The tape should start halfway between the first and second creases, extending to halfway between the second and third creases.  3. **Wrap the Tape**: Bend your finger about 60\u00b0 at the joint. Wrap one end of the split tape around the upper part of the finger, forming a ring. Wrap the other end below the joint, creating another ring.  4. **Overlap**: Ensure the tape overlaps by 50% for added strength and support.  5. **Retape**: Since tape stretches quickly, retape between climbing routes to maintain support.  This method also serves as a psychological reminder not to overexert your finger.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 4.881952237549085, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": 4.60863476087168, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 4.4105473123243, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": 4.397768729316399, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:4": 4.338728992481166, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": 4.235119306812918, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 4.183457558889976, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 4.134992213075861, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": 4.07581615364576, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 4.06814597695852}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 4.88195223